.Sh DESCRIPTION
The Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) enables applications to
determine the (revocation) state of an identified certificate (RFC
2560).
.Pp
This command performs many common OCSP tasks.
It can be used to print out requests and responses, create requests and
send queries to an OCSP responder and behave like a mini OCSP server
itself.
.Sh OPTIONS
This command operates as either a client or a server.
The options are described below, divided into those two modes.
.Ss OCSP Client Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -out Em filename
specify output filename, default is standard output.
.It Sy -issuer Em filename
This specifies the current issuer certificate.
The input can be in PEM, DER, or PKCS#12 format.
.Pp
This option can be used multiple times.
This option
.Sy MUST
come before any
.Sy -cert
options.
.It Sy -cert Em filename
Add the certificate
.Em filename
to the request.
The input can be in PEM, DER, or PKCS#12 format.
.Pp
This option can be used multiple times.
The issuer certificate is taken from the previous
.Sy -issuer
option, or an error occurs if no issuer certificate is specified.
.It Sy -no_certs
Don't include any certificates in signed request.
.It Sy -serial Em num
Same as the
.Sy -cert
option except the certificate with serial number
.Sy num
is added to the request.
The serial number is interpreted as a decimal integer unless preceded by
.Qo Li 0x Qc .
Negative integers can also be specified by preceding the value by a
.Qo Li - Qc
sign.
.It Sy -signer Em filename Ns , Sy -signkey Em filename
Sign the OCSP request using the certificate specified in the
.Sy -signer
option and the private key specified by the
.Sy -signkey
option.
The input can be in PEM, DER, or PKCS#12 format.
.Pp
If the
.Sy -signkey
option is not present then the private key is read from the same file as
the certificate.
If neither option is specified then the OCSP request is not signed.
.It Sy -sign_other Em filename
Additional certificates to include in the signed request.
The input can be in PEM, DER, or PKCS#12 format.
.It Sy -nonce Ns , Sy -no_nonce
Add an OCSP nonce extension to a request or disable OCSP nonce addition.
Normally if an OCSP request is input using the
.Sy -reqin
option no nonce is added: using the
.Sy -nonce
option will force addition of a nonce.
If an OCSP request is being created (using
.Sy -cert
and
.Sy -serial
options) a nonce is automatically added specifying
.Sy -no_nonce
overrides this.
.It Sy -req_text Ns , Sy -resp_text Ns , Sy -text
Print out the text form of the OCSP request, response or both
respectively.
.It Sy -reqout Em file Ns , Sy -respout Em filename
Write out the DER-encoded OCSP request or response to
.Em filename .
The output filename can be the same as the input filename, which leads
to replacing the file contents.
Note that file I/O is not atomic.
The output file is truncated and then written.
.It Sy -reqin Em file Ns , Sy -respin Em filename
Read OCSP request or response file from
.Em file .
These option are ignored if OCSP request or response creation is implied
by other options (for example with
.Sy -serial ,
.Sy -cert
and
.Sy -host
options).
.It Sy -url Em responder_url
Specify the responder host and optionally port and path via a URL. Both
HTTP and HTTPS (SSL/TLS) URLs can be specified.
The optional userinfo and fragment components are ignored.
Any given query component is handled as part of the path component.
For details, see the
.Sy -host
and
.Sy -path
options described next.
.It Sy -host Em host Ns : Ns Em port Ns , Sy -path Em pathname
If the
.Sy -host
option is present then the OCSP request is sent to the host
.Em host
on port
.Em port .
The
.Em host
may be a domain name or an IP (v4 or v6) address, such as
.Qo Li 127.0.0.1 Qc
or
.Qo Li [::1] Qc
for localhost.
If it is an IPv6 address, it must be enclosed in
.Qo Li [ Qc
and
.Qo Li ] Qc .
.Pp
The
.Sy -path
option specifies the HTTP pathname to use or "/" by default.
This is equivalent to specifying
.Sy -url
with scheme http:// and the given
.Em host ,
.Em port ,
and optional
.Em pathname .
.It Sy -proxy Em [http[s]://][userinfo@]host[:port][/path][?query][#fragment]
The HTTP(S) proxy server to use for reaching the OCSP server unless
.Sy -no_proxy
applies, see below.
If the host string is an IPv6 address, it must be enclosed in
.Qo Li [ Qc
and
.Qo Li ] Qc .
The proxy port defaults to 80 or 443 if the scheme is
.Qo Li https Qc ;
apart from that the optional
.Qo Li http:// Qc
or
.Qo Li https:// Qc
prefix is ignored, as well as any userinfo, path, query, and fragment
components.
Defaults to the environment variable
.Qo Li http_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li HTTP_PROXY Qc
in case no TLS is used, otherwise
.Qo Li https_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li HTTPS_PROXY Qc .
.It Sy -no_proxy Em addresses
List of IP addresses and/or DNS names of servers not to use an HTTP(S)
proxy for, separated by commas and/or whitespace (where in the latter
case the whole argument must be enclosed in "..."). Default is from the
environment variable
.Qo Li no_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li NO_PROXY Qc .
.It Sy -header Em name Ns = Ns Em value
Adds the header
.Em name
with the specified
.Em value
to the OCSP request that is sent to the responder.
This may be repeated.
.It Sy -timeout Em seconds
Connection timeout to the OCSP responder in seconds.
On POSIX systems, when running as an OCSP responder, this option also
limits the time that the responder is willing to wait for the client
request.
This time is measured from the time the responder accepts the connection
until the complete request is received.
.It Sy -verify_other Em file
File or URI containing additional certificates to search when attempting
to locate the OCSP response signing certificate.
Some responders omit the actual signer's certificate from the response:
this option can be used to supply the necessary certificate in such
cases.
The input can be in PEM, DER, or PKCS#12 format.
.It Sy -trust_other
The certificates specified by the
.Sy -verify_other
option should be explicitly trusted and no additional checks will be
performed on them.
This is useful when the complete responder certificate chain is not
available or trusting a root CA is not appropriate.
.It Sy -VAfile Em file
File or URI containing explicitly trusted responder certificates.
Equivalent to the
.Sy -verify_other
and
.Sy -trust_other
options.
The input can be in PEM, DER, or PKCS#12 format.
.It Sy -noverify
Don't attempt to verify the OCSP response signature or the nonce values.
This option will normally only be used for debugging since it disables
all verification of the responders certificate.
.It Sy -no_intern
Ignore certificates contained in the OCSP response when searching for
the signers certificate.
With this option the signers certificate must be specified with either
the
.Sy -verify_other
or
.Sy -VAfile
options.
.It Sy -no_signature_verify
Don't check the signature on the OCSP response.
Since this option tolerates invalid signatures on OCSP responses it will
normally only be used for testing purposes.
.It Sy -no_cert_verify
Don't verify the OCSP response signers certificate at all.
Since this option allows the OCSP response to be signed by any
certificate it should only be used for testing purposes.
.It Sy -no_chain
Do not use certificates in the response as additional untrusted CA
certificates.
.It Sy -no_explicit
Do not explicitly trust the root CA if it is set to be trusted for OCSP
signing.
.It Sy -no_cert_checks
Don't perform any additional checks on the OCSP response signers
certificate.
That is do not make any checks to see if the signers certificate is
authorised to provide the necessary status information: as a result this
option should only be used for testing purposes.
.It Sy -validity_period Em nsec Ns , Sy -status_age Em age
These options specify the range of times, in seconds, which will be
tolerated in an OCSP response.
Each certificate status response includes a
.Sy notBefore
time and an optional
.Sy notAfter
time.
The current time should fall between these two values, but the interval
between the two times may be only a few seconds.
In practice the OCSP responder and clients clocks may not be precisely
synchronised and so such a check may fail.
To avoid this the
.Sy -validity_period
option can be used to specify an acceptable error range in seconds, the
default value is 5 minutes.
.Pp
If the
.Sy notAfter
time is omitted from a response then this means that new status
information is immediately available.
In this case the age of the
.Sy notBefore
field is checked to see it is not older than
.Em age
seconds old.
By default this additional check is not performed.
.It Sy -rcid Em digest
This option sets the digest algorithm to use for certificate
identification in the OCSP response.
Any digest supported by the
.Xr openssl-dgst 1
command can be used.
The default is the same digest algorithm used in the request.
.It Sy - Ns Em digest
This option sets digest algorithm to use for certificate identification
in the OCSP request.
Any digest supported by the OpenSSL
.Sy dgst
command can be used.
The default is SHA-1. This option may be used multiple times to specify
the digest used by subsequent certificate identifiers.
.It Sy -CAfile Em file Ns , Sy -no-CAfile Ns , Sy -CApath Em dir Ns , Sy -no-CApath Ns , Sy -CAstore Em uri Ns , Sy -no-CAstore
See
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -allow_proxy_certs Ns , Sy -attime Ns , Sy -no_check_time Ns , Sy -check_ss_sig Ns , Sy -crl_check Ns , Sy -crl_check_all Ns , Sy -explicit_policy Ns , Sy -extended_crl Ns , Sy -ignore_critical Ns , Sy -inhibit_any Ns , Sy -inhibit_map Ns , Sy -no_alt_chains Ns , Sy -partial_chain Ns , Sy -policy Ns , Sy -policy_check Ns , Sy -policy_print Ns , Sy -purpose Ns , Sy -suiteB_128 Ns , Sy -suiteB_128_only Ns , Sy -suiteB_192 Ns , Sy -trusted_first Ns , Sy -use_deltas Ns , Sy -auth_level Ns , Sy -verify_depth Ns , Sy -verify_email Ns , Sy -verify_hostname Ns , Sy -verify_ip Ns , Sy -verify_name Ns , Sy -x509_strict Sy -issuer_checks
Set various options of certificate chain verification.
See
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.El
.Ss OCSP Server Options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -index Em indexfile
The
.Em indexfile
parameter is the name of a text index file in
.Sy ca
format containing certificate revocation information.
.Pp
If the
.Sy -index
option is specified then this command switches to responder mode,
otherwise it is in client mode.
The request(s) the responder processes can be either specified on the
command line (using
.Sy -issuer
and
.Sy -serial
options), supplied in a file (using the
.Sy -reqin
option) or via external OCSP clients (if
.Sy -port
or
.Sy -url
is specified).
.Pp
If the
.Sy -index
option is present then the
.Sy -CA
and
.Sy -rsigner
options must also be present.
.It Sy -CA Em file
CA certificates corresponding to the revocation information in the index
file given with
.Sy -index .
The input can be in PEM, DER, or PKCS#12 format.
.It Sy -rsigner Em file
The certificate to sign OCSP responses with.
The input can be in PEM, DER, or PKCS#12 format.
.It Sy -rkey Em file
The private key to sign OCSP responses with: if not present the file
specified in the
.Sy -rsigner
option is used.
.It Sy -passin Em arg
The private key password source.
For more information about the format of
.Em arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -rother Em file
Additional certificates to include in the OCSP response.
The input can be in PEM, DER, or PKCS#12 format.
.It Sy -rsigopt Em nm Ns : Ns Em v
Pass options to the signature algorithm when signing OCSP responses.
Names and values of these options are algorithm-specific.
.It Sy -rmd Em digest
The digest to use when signing the response.
.It Sy -badsig
Corrupt the response signature before writing it; this can be useful for
testing.
.It Sy -resp_no_certs
Don't include any certificates in the OCSP response.
.It Sy -resp_key_id
Identify the signer certificate using the key ID, default is to use the
subject name.
.It Sy -port Em portnum
Port to listen for OCSP requests on.
Both IPv4 and IPv6 are possible.
The port may also be specified using the
.Sy -url
option.
A
.Qo Li 0 Qc
argument indicates that any available port shall be chosen
automatically.
.It Sy -ignore_err
Ignore malformed requests or responses: When acting as an OCSP client,
retry if a malformed response is received.
When acting as an OCSP responder, continue running instead of
terminating upon receiving a malformed request.
.It Sy -nrequest Em number
The OCSP server will exit after receiving
.Em number
requests, default unlimited.
.It Sy -multi Em process-count
Run the specified number of OCSP responder child processes, with the
parent process respawning child processes as needed.
Child processes will detect changes in the CA index file and
automatically reload it.
When running as a responder
.Sy -timeout
option is recommended to limit the time each child is willing to wait
for the client's OCSP response.
This option is available on POSIX systems (that support the
.Xr fork 3
and other required unix system-calls).
.It Sy -nmin Em minutes Ns , Sy -ndays Em days
Number of minutes or days when fresh revocation information is
available: used in the
.Sy nextUpdate
field.
If neither option is present then the
.Sy nextUpdate
field is omitted meaning fresh revocation information is immediately
available.
.El
.Sh OCSP RESPONSE VERIFICATION
OCSP Response follows the rules specified in RFC2560.
.Pp
Initially the OCSP responder certificate is located and the signature on
the OCSP request checked using the responder certificate's public key.
.Pp
Then a normal certificate verify is performed on the OCSP responder
certificate building up a certificate chain in the process.
The locations of the trusted certificates used to build the chain can be
specified by the
.Sy -CAfile ,
.Sy -CApath
or
.Sy -CAstore
options or they will be looked for in the standard OpenSSL certificates
directory.
.Pp
If the initial verify fails then the OCSP verify process halts with an
error.
.Pp
Otherwise the issuing CA certificate in the request is compared to the
OCSP responder certificate: if there is a match then the OCSP verify
succeeds.
.Pp
Otherwise the OCSP responder certificate's CA is checked against the
issuing CA certificate in the request.
If there is a match and the OCSPSigning extended key usage is present in
the OCSP responder certificate then the OCSP verify succeeds.
.Pp
Otherwise, if
.Sy -no_explicit
is
.Sy not
set the root CA of the OCSP responders CA is checked to see if it is
trusted for OCSP signing.
If it is the OCSP verify succeeds.
.Pp
If none of these checks is successful then the OCSP verify fails.
.Pp
What this effectively means if that if the OCSP responder certificate is
authorised directly by the CA it is issuing revocation information about
(and it is correctly configured) then verification will succeed.
.Pp
If the OCSP responder is a "global responder" which can give details
about multiple CAs and has its own separate certificate chain then its
root CA can be trusted for OCSP signing.
For example:
.Bd -literal
 openssl x509 -in ocspCA.pem -addtrust OCSPSigning -out trustedCA.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively the responder certificate itself can be explicitly trusted
with the
.Sy -VAfile
option.
.Sh NOTES
As noted, most of the verify options are for testing or debugging
purposes.
Normally only the
.Sy -CApath ,
.Sy -CAfile ,
.Sy -CAstore
and (if the responder is a 'global VA')
.Sy -VAfile
options need to be used.
.Pp
The OCSP server is only useful for test and demonstration purposes: it
is not really usable as a full OCSP responder.
It contains only a very simple HTTP request handling and can only handle
the POST form of OCSP queries.
It also handles requests serially meaning it cannot respond to new
requests until it has processed the current one.
The text index file format of revocation is also inefficient for large
quantities of revocation data.
.Pp
It is possible to run this command in responder mode via a CGI script
using the
.Sy -reqin
and
.Sy -respout
options.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create an OCSP request and write it to a file:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ocsp -issuer issuer.pem -cert c1.pem -cert c2.pem -reqout req.der
.Ed
.Pp
Send a query to an OCSP responder with URL http://ocsp.myhost.com/ save
the response to a file, print it out in text form, and verify the
response:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ocsp -issuer issuer.pem -cert c1.pem -cert c2.pem \e
     -url http://ocsp.myhost.com/ -resp_text -respout resp.der
.Ed
.Pp
Read in an OCSP response and print out text form:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ocsp -respin resp.der -text -noverify
.Ed
.Pp
OCSP server on port 8888 using a standard
.Sy ca
configuration, and a separate responder certificate.
All requests and responses are printed to a file.
.Bd -literal
 openssl ocsp -index demoCA/index.txt -port 8888 -rsigner rcert.pem -CA demoCA/cacert.pem
        -text -out log.txt
.Ed
.Pp
As above but exit after processing one request:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ocsp -index demoCA/index.txt -port 8888 -rsigner rcert.pem -CA demoCA/cacert.pem
     -nrequest 1
.Ed
.Pp
Query status information using an internally generated request:
.Bd -literal
 openssl ocsp -index demoCA/index.txt -rsigner rcert.pem -CA demoCA/cacert.pem
     -issuer demoCA/cacert.pem -serial 1
.Ed
.Pp
Query status information using request read from a file, and write the
response to a second file.
.Bd -literal
 openssl ocsp -index demoCA/index.txt -rsigner rcert.pem -CA demoCA/cacert.pem
     -reqin req.der -respout resp.der
.Ed
